  "answer": "This is a storefront cart quantity limit. I'll follow the feature playbook and start by reading it and the shopizer cart API.The cart limit spans storefront endpoints and merchant config. I'll map those two slices before changing anything.The limit belongs in one place, checked before any cart line is saved. I'll add that guard on the create, add, and update paths.A storefront cart line is now limited by the store merchant configuration key `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY`, and the limit is 99 when that key is missing. `POST /api/v1/cart`, `PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}`, and `POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi` read the value from the database on each request and reject a line above the cap with HTTP 400 before the cart is saved. A quantity of 0 still removes the line. Model the Domain put the cap in `CartLineQuantityLimit`. Boundary Discipline parses the setting there and checks it before any line changes. Laziness Protocol kept that one rule on the three write paths in `ShoppingCartFacadeImpl`.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am test -Dtest=CartLineQuantityLimitTest -DfailIfNoTests=false\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 on the three cart endpoints and that a rejected request leaves the stored cart unchanged\"], \"missing\": [\"a running shop with a store fixture and a documented integration test for CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY\"]}The three explorations match the cart change already in place. [Cart REST endpoints](d543ef27-eeb0-49a8-9f7d-02797df841b9) shows the quantity writes are `POST /api/v1/cart`, `PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}`, and `POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi`, and that a rejection stays off the cart only if it happens before `deleteShoppingCartItem` and `saveShoppingCart`. The cap is checked there. [Merchant config keys](184fb488-0e96-4884-9d5b-4b37310b8cf7) shows `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` is a per-request database read with no cache, which is how the setting is loaded. [Cart quantity validation](940816e4-3ca6-4396-930c-40ace297e2b5) shows HTTP 400 comes from `ServiceRuntimeException`, which is what an over-cap line throws. No further change.",
